# 14. AI API CONFIGURATION

Dev Hub must support an external AI provider through a secure backend integration.

An administrator should be able to configure the AI provider and API key.

### AI settings

Create an Admin Settings area where authorized administrators can:

- Select the supported AI provider.
- Enter or update the API key.
- Test the connection.
- View connection status.
- Configure the model where supported.
- Set usage limits.
- Monitor API usage and estimated costs.
- View error logs.

### Security requirements

- Never expose API keys in frontend code.
- Never store secret keys in browser local storage.
- Store credentials securely using backend environment variables or a suitable secrets manager.
- Ensure only authorized administrators can configure AI credentials.
- Do not send private member information to the AI provider unless necessary for the requested feature.
- Use server-side validation and access controls.
- Handle rate limits, provider errors, timeouts and failed requests gracefully.

### AI architecture

Build the AI integration so that different features can use consistent, reusable services.

Separate the AI functionality into logical operations:

- Roadmap generation.
- Learning plan generation.
- Daily exercise generation.
- Code review.
- Quiz generation.
- Quiz evaluation.
- Weekly project generation.
- Project review.
- AI mentoring.
- Weekly progress summaries.

Do not put all AI logic into one enormous prompt or one frontend component.

Each operation should receive the correct member context and return structured, validated results.

