# 18. WORDING AND CONTENT DESIGN

This requirement is extremely important.

Every part of Dev Hub must be understandable to someone completely new to development.

Use plain, friendly and direct language.

Avoid unnecessary technical or corporate terminology.

Examples:

Instead of "Learning Analytics", use "Your Progress".

Instead of "Curriculum Architecture", use "Your Learning Plan".

Instead of "Knowledge Assessment", use "Test Your Understanding".

Instead of "Submission Evaluation", use "Code Review".

Instead of "Skill Acquisition", use "Skills You're Learning".

Instead of "Resource Repository", use "Learning Resources".

Instead of "Learning Trajectory", use "Your Roadmap".

Use short sentences.

Make buttons describe exactly what happens when clicked.

Examples:
- Start Learning.
- Continue Learning.
- Add a Course.
- Submit My Code.
- Take Quiz.
- View Feedback.
- Start Project.
- Ask AI Mentor.

Empty states should explain what the member can do next.

Error messages should explain what happened and how to fix it.

Avoid giant paragraphs, confusing labels and unnecessary instructions.

**Golden rule: If a beginner has to ask what a button or section means, simplify it.**

